language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ knights_tour: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ A knight's tour is a sequence of moves of a knight on a chessboard such that the knight visits every square exactly once, without returning to its starting square. Find a knight's tour on an n by n chessboard, giving for each square the number of the move at which the knight visits it. The moves are numbered from 0 to n*n-1.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `knights_tour.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/knights_tour.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `knights_tour_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py knights_tour.json
$   savilerow model.eprime knights_tour_1.param -run-solver

$ n: the size of the chessboard, which has n rows and n columns
given n : int

$ the number of squares, n*n; the moves are numbered from 0 to cells-1
letting cells = n * n

$ rowOf[t], colOf[t]: row and column (1-based) of the square visited at move t-1 (t is 1-based, so rowOf[1] is move 0)
find rowOf : matrix indexed by [int(1..cells)] of int(1..n)
find colOf : matrix indexed by [int(1..cells)] of int(1..n)
$ squareOf[t]: the square of move t-1 numbered row by row, (row-1)*n + column, from 1 to cells
find squareOf : matrix indexed by [int(1..cells)] of int(1..cells)
$ moveOfSquare[s]: the move number (0-based) at which square s is visited, where s = (row-1)*n + column
find moveOfSquare : matrix indexed by [int(1..cells)] of int(0..cells-1)
$ x[r,c]: the number of the move at which the knight visits the square in row r and column c (rows and columns indexed from 1 here; moves numbered from 0)
find x : matrix indexed by [int(1..n), int(1..n)] of int(0..cells-1)

$ search: follow the tour move by move, so each move is chosen next to the one before it
branching on [squareOf]

such that
$ the square of each move is given by its row and column
forAll t : int(1..cells) . squareOf[t] = (rowOf[t] - 1) * n + colOf[t],
$ every square is visited at a different move, so the squares of the moves are all different
allDiff(squareOf),
$ the move after move t is a knight's move (two squares in one direction and one in the other) from the square of move t
forAll t : int(1..cells-1) . (((|rowOf[t] - rowOf[t+1]| = 1) /\ (|colOf[t] - colOf[t+1]| = 2)) \/ ((|rowOf[t] - rowOf[t+1]| = 2) /\ (|colOf[t] - colOf[t+1]| = 1))),
$ moveOfSquare gives the move number of each square, so the move number of the square of move t-1 is t-1
forAll t : int(1..cells) . moveOfSquare[squareOf[t]] = t - 1,
$ x[r,c] is the move number of the square in row r and column c
forAll r : int(1..n) . forAll c : int(1..n) . x[r,c] = moveOfSquare[(r - 1) * n + c],
$ symmetry breaking: the start square lies in the top-left triangle of the board (the tour is reflected or transposed to fit, which keeps it a tour)
2 * rowOf[1] <= n + 1,
colOf[1] <= rowOf[1]
